select 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-00000000000e', v.pos, v.title, v.kind, v.body::jsonb, v.notes, v.mins
from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: how much medieval history do you know?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Historians usually date the start of the Middle Ages to the fall of the Western Roman Empire in…","options":["323 CE","476 CE","800 CE","1066 CE"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In 476 CE the Germanic commander Odoacer deposed the last Western emperor, Romulus Augustulus — a traditional end point for Rome in the west."},{"prompt":"The Frankish king crowned Emperor by the pope on Christmas Day in the year 800 was…","options":["Clovis","Charlemagne","Charles Martel","Otto I"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pope Leo III crowned Charlemagne (Charles the Great) in Rome in 800 CE, reviving an imperial title in the west."},{"prompt":"In the feudal relationship, a lord granted a vassal land (a fief) in exchange for…","options":["gold coins","military service and loyalty","a share of the harvest only","prayers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A vassal swore homage and fealty and served the lord, usually as a mounted warrior, in return for the fief."},{"prompt":"The capital of the Byzantine (Eastern Roman) Empire was…","options":["Rome","Ravenna","Constantinople","Athens"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Constantinople, founded by Constantine in 330 CE, was the Byzantine capital until it fell in 1453."},{"prompt":"The pandemic that killed perhaps a third of Europe''s people around 1347-1351 is called…","options":["the Great Famine","the Black Death","the Antonine Plague","the Spanish flu"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Black Death, a wave of plague, devastated Europe in the mid-1300s."},{"prompt":"The 1215 English charter that limited the king''s power and is seen as a milestone for the rule of law is the…","options":["Domesday Book","Magna Carta","Treaty of Verdun","Edict of Milan"],"answer":1,"explanation":"King John sealed Magna Carta (''Great Charter'') at Runnymede in 1215 under pressure from his barons."},{"prompt":"The series of religious wars begun when Pope Urban II called for the recovery of Jerusalem in 1095 are the…","options":["Hundred Years'' War","Crusades","Wars of the Roses","Reconquista"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At the Council of Clermont in 1095 Urban II launched the First Crusade; Jerusalem was taken in 1099."},{"prompt":"Gothic cathedrals of the High Middle Ages are known for pointed arches, ribbed vaults, and…","options":["flat timber roofs","flying buttresses and stained glass","domed concrete ceilings","mud-brick walls"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Flying buttresses carried the weight outward, letting walls open up for tall stained-glass windows."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'The fall of Rome and the Early Middle Ages', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain how and why the Western Roman Empire came apart, and describe the world that replaced it in the Early Middle Ages (about 500 to 1000 CE). Warm-up: from the diagnostic, which year do historians traditionally give for the fall of Rome in the west? Hold that date in mind. By the end of this lesson you should be able to name two pressures that strained the empire from within and two that pressed on it from outside, and to say why the centuries that followed were once nicknamed the ''Dark Ages'' even though a great deal was happening. You will also be able to place the collapse not as a single dramatic event but as a slow unraveling that stretched across generations, so that a person living through it might not have realized an age was ending."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Rome did not fall in a day. The empire split for good in 395 CE into western and eastern halves. The west faced overstretched borders, heavy taxes, civil wars, shrinking trade, and a currency losing value, while waves of peoples moved into its lands during the Migration Period. Pushed partly by the Huns, groups such as the Visigoths, Vandals, and Ostrogoths crossed the frontiers. The Visigoths sacked Rome in 410 CE and the Vandals again in 455 CE. In 476 CE the commander Odoacer deposed the boy-emperor Romulus Augustulus, and no new western emperor replaced him. What followed was not emptiness but reorganization: smaller Germanic kingdoms, a countryside that mattered more than cities, and a Christian Church that increasingly supplied order, learning, and record-keeping where imperial administration had withdrawn."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"395 CE: Theodosius I dies and the empire is permanently divided between his sons. 410 CE: Alaric and the Visigoths sack the city of Rome, shocking the Roman world. 451-453 CE: Attila the Hun ravages the west before dying, loosening the Hunnic threat. 455 CE: the Vandals sack Rome a second time. 476 CE: Odoacer removes Romulus Augustulus, the conventional end of the Western Empire. Meanwhile the Eastern (Byzantine) Empire survived and even tried to reconquer the west under Justinian in the 500s. Remember that ''fall'' means the loss of central imperial government in the west, not the disappearance of Roman law, language, roads, or religion, which shaped Europe for centuries. Latin lived on in the Church and seeded the Romance languages."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In what year is the Western Roman Empire traditionally said to have fallen? 2) Name one internal weakness and one external pressure on the late empire. 3) Who deposed the last western emperor, and what was that emperor''s name? 4) Did the Eastern Roman Empire also fall in 476 CE? 5) Why is ''Dark Ages'' a misleading label for this period? Answers: 1) 476 CE. 2) Internal: heavy taxation, civil war, or economic decline; external: migrations and invasions (Visigoths, Vandals, Huns). 3) Odoacer deposed Romulus Augustulus. 4) No — the Eastern (Byzantine) Empire continued for nearly another thousand years, until 1453. 5) Learning, law, and faith continued through the Church and monasteries; the term undervalues real activity and change."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: imagining Rome was destroyed overnight by a single invasion — the decline took centuries and had many causes. Mistake: thinking the whole Roman world ended in 476 CE; the eastern half thrived on. Mistake: picturing the Early Middle Ages as totally stagnant; it was a time of new kingdoms, Christianization, and preserved learning. Exit ticket: (a) What single year marks the traditional fall of the Western Roman Empire? (b) Which institution increasingly provided order and literacy as imperial rule faded? Answers: (a) 476 CE. (b) The Christian Church, especially through bishops and monasteries that copied texts and kept records."}]}'::jsonb, 'CE means ''Common Era'' and counts forward from year 1; a smaller CE number is earlier. The Early Middle Ages run roughly 500-1000 CE.', 13),
  (3, 'The Franks and Charlemagne''s empire', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Trace how the Franks became the strongest power in western Europe, and explain why Charlemagne''s coronation in 800 CE mattered. Warm-up: the diagnostic named the ruler crowned by the pope on Christmas Day 800 — recall who he was. After this lesson you should be able to distinguish the Merovingian from the Carolingian dynasty, describe what the Carolingian Renaissance achieved, and explain how the empire was divided after Charlemagne''s death. You will be able to argue that Charlemagne''s reign briefly reunited much of western Europe under one crown and tied royal power to the Church, setting patterns that outlasted his empire by centuries."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"After Rome, the Franks built the most durable Germanic kingdom, in what is now France and Germany. Their first dynasty, the Merovingians, began with Clovis I, who united the Franks and converted to Catholic Christianity around 500 CE — a choice that bound his people to the Church of Rome. Real power later shifted to officials called mayors of the palace. One of them, Charles Martel, halted a Muslim advance at the Battle of Tours in 732 CE. His descendants, the Carolingians, took the crown outright: Pepin the Short was anointed king in 751 CE with papal blessing. Pepin''s son Charlemagne conquered widely, promoted learning and church reform, and in 800 CE was crowned Emperor by Pope Leo III, reviving an imperial title in the Latin west and knitting throne and altar together."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"c. 496-508 CE: Clovis I converts and consolidates Frankish power. 732 CE: Charles Martel defeats an Umayyad raiding force near Tours, checking expansion into Gaul. 751 CE: Pepin the Short deposes the last Merovingian and is anointed, founding Carolingian rule. 768-814 CE: Charlemagne reigns, campaigning against Saxons, Lombards, and others, and making Aachen his capital. 800 CE: Pope Leo III crowns him ''Emperor of the Romans'' in Rome. Charlemagne sponsored the Carolingian Renaissance, gathering scholars like Alcuin of York, standardizing a clear script, and reviving schools. 843 CE: the Treaty of Verdun splits the empire among his three grandsons, creating west, east, and middle kingdoms whose borders foreshadow later France and Germany."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which Frankish king converted to Catholic Christianity around 500 CE? 2) What did Charles Martel accomplish in 732 CE? 3) In what year was Charlemagne crowned Emperor, and by whom? 4) What was the Carolingian Renaissance? 5) What did the Treaty of Verdun (843 CE) do? Answers: 1) Clovis I, founder of the Merovingian line. 2) He defeated a Muslim army at the Battle of Tours, checking its advance into Frankish lands. 3) 800 CE, by Pope Leo III. 4) A revival of learning, education, and manuscript copying under Charlemagne''s patronage. 5) It divided Charlemagne''s empire among his three grandsons into roughly western, middle, and eastern kingdoms."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: confusing the two dynasties — the Merovingians came first (Clovis), the Carolingians second (Charles Martel, Pepin, Charlemagne). Mistake: thinking Charlemagne''s empire lasted — it fractured within a generation of his death. Mistake: dating the Battle of Tours or the coronation loosely; Tours is 732 CE, the coronation 800 CE. Exit ticket: (a) Name the two Frankish dynasties in order. (b) What happened to Charlemagne''s empire in 843 CE? Answers: (a) Merovingians, then Carolingians. (b) The Treaty of Verdun split it three ways among his grandsons."}]}'::jsonb, 'A dynasty is a line of rulers from one family. The Franks had two great ones: the Merovingians, then the Carolingians named for Charlemagne.', 14),
  (4, 'Feudalism and the manor', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe the two linked systems that organized much of medieval society: feudalism, which bound warriors and lords, and manorialism, which bound peasants to the land. Warm-up: the diagnostic asked what a vassal gave a lord in return for a fief — recall the answer. By the end you will be able to explain the ladder of obligation from king down to knight, define homage and fealty, and describe daily life on a manor, including the roles of the lord, the peasants, and the serfs. You will also be able to explain why this decentralized order spread after the Carolingian empire broke apart and central authority weakened."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"With strong central government gone, protection and land became the glue of society. Historians describe the result with two words. Feudalism names the web of ties among the warrior elite: a lord granted land, called a fief, to a vassal, who in return swore homage and fealty and served the lord, usually as a mounted knight. A great lord was himself the vassal of someone higher, up to the king. Manorialism names the economic base beneath them. A manor was a self-contained estate where peasants worked the land. Free peasants paid rent; serfs were legally bound to the manor, owing labor on the lord''s own fields (the demesne) and a share of their crops, and were not free to leave. In exchange they received land to farm and the lord''s protection."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"This was a structure more than a dated sequence, taking shape across the 800s and 900s CE as Viking, Magyar, and Muslim raids made local defense urgent. Key roles: the king at the top; great nobles (dukes and counts) holding large fiefs; knights, the armored cavalry who did much of the fighting; and at the base the vast majority, the peasants. The ceremony of homage, in which a vassal knelt and placed his hands between the lord''s, sealed the bond, followed by an oath of fealty (faithfulness). Farming improved over time with the heavy plow, the three-field system that rested one field each year, and the horse collar, which slowly raised yields and supported a growing population by the High Middle Ages."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What is a fief? 2) What two things did a vassal owe a lord? 3) What is the difference between a free peasant and a serf? 4) What was the demesne? 5) Why did feudal arrangements spread in the 800s and 900s CE? Answers: 1) A grant of land from a lord to a vassal. 2) Military service (and counsel) plus loyalty, sealed by homage and fealty. 3) A free peasant paid rent and could move; a serf was legally bound to the manor and could not leave. 4) The lord''s own portion of the manor''s land, worked by the peasants. 5) Weak central power and raids by Vikings, Magyars, and others made local lords the main source of protection."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating feudalism and manorialism as the same thing — feudalism links lords and warriors over land and service, while manorialism organizes peasant labor and farming on an estate. Mistake: thinking all peasants were slaves; serfs were unfree but had rights, land to work, and protection, unlike chattel slaves. Mistake: imagining a tidy pyramid everywhere; arrangements varied widely by region and time. Exit ticket: (a) Define a vassal. (b) Name one farming improvement that raised yields in this period. Answers: (a) Someone who received a fief and owed a lord service and loyalty. (b) The heavy plow, the three-field system, or the horse collar."}]}'::jsonb, 'A fief is the land a lord grants; a vassal is the person who receives it and owes service. A serf is a peasant bound to the manor''s land.', 13),
  (5, 'The Church, monasticism, and the papacy', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain why the Christian Church was the most powerful institution in medieval western Europe, describe the role of monasteries, and summarize how the papacy grew in authority. Warm-up: think back to Lesson 2 — which institution took over much of Rome''s record-keeping and learning after the empire withdrew? After this lesson you will be able to describe the Rule of Saint Benedict and life in a monastery, explain how monks preserved ancient texts, and outline the Investiture Controversy, the struggle between popes and kings over who could appoint church officials. You will be able to argue that the Church shaped not only belief but also law, learning, politics, and daily life across the whole medieval west."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Nearly everyone in western Europe was Christian, and the Church reached into every village. It baptized, married, and buried people, ran schools, cared for the poor and sick, and taught a shared view of the world. Monasteries were its engine of learning. Around 529 CE Benedict of Nursia wrote a Rule balancing prayer and work (''ora et labora'') that became the standard for western monks. In their scriptoria, monks copied scripture and classical texts by hand, preserving much of what survives from antiquity. The papacy, the office of the pope in Rome, slowly claimed leadership over the western Church. Popes such as Gregory the Great (590-604 CE) strengthened the office, and reformers later insisted that the Church, not kings, should govern its own affairs — a claim that set up a historic clash."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"c. 529 CE: Benedict founds Monte Cassino and writes his Rule. 590-604 CE: Pope Gregory the Great expands papal influence and sponsors missions. 910 CE: the monastery of Cluny is founded, launching a reform movement for stricter, independent monasticism. 1054 CE: the Great Schism splits Christianity into the Roman Catholic west and the Eastern Orthodox east over doctrine and papal authority. 1075-1122 CE: the Investiture Controversy pits Pope Gregory VII against Emperor Henry IV over who appoints bishops; in a famous scene at Canossa in 1077 CE, Henry stood in the snow seeking the pope''s pardon. The dispute was finally settled by the Concordat of Worms in 1122 CE. Monks and nuns, bishops, and popes together made the Church a parallel power to kings."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What did the Rule of Saint Benedict emphasize? 2) How did monasteries help preserve ancient knowledge? 3) What split Christianity into Catholic and Orthodox branches in 1054 CE? 4) What was the Investiture Controversy about? 5) What happened at Canossa in 1077 CE? Answers: 1) A balance of prayer and work (''ora et labora'') under a common Rule. 2) Monks copied scripture and classical texts by hand in their scriptoria. 3) The Great Schism of 1054 CE, dividing the Roman Catholic west from the Eastern Orthodox east. 4) Whether kings or the Church had the right to appoint bishops and other church officials. 5) Emperor Henry IV publicly sought Pope Gregory VII''s pardon, a dramatic sign of papal power."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking the pope always controlled all of Christianity — the eastern Church broke with Rome in 1054 CE and the papacy''s western authority grew gradually and was often contested. Mistake: picturing monasteries only as places of prayer; they were also centers of farming, medicine, and the copying of books. Mistake: confusing the Investiture Controversy with the East-West Schism; the first is popes versus kings, the second is Catholic versus Orthodox. Exit ticket: (a) Who wrote the influential monastic Rule around 529 CE? (b) What did the Investiture Controversy dispute? Answers: (a) Benedict of Nursia. (b) Whether the Church or secular rulers could appoint bishops."}]}'::jsonb, 'Monasticism is the way of life of monks and nuns in monasteries; the papacy is the office and authority of the pope, the bishop of Rome.', 14),
  (6, 'Checkpoint 1: from Rome to the year 1000', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The Western Roman Empire traditionally fell when Odoacer deposed Romulus Augustulus in…","options":["410 CE","476 CE","751 CE","843 CE"],"answer":1,"explanation":"476 CE is the conventional end of the Western Roman Empire; 410 CE was the Visigothic sack of Rome."},{"prompt":"The battle of 732 CE in which Charles Martel checked a Muslim advance into Frankish lands was the Battle of…","options":["Hastings","Tours","Manzikert","Agincourt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At Tours (also called Poitiers) in 732 CE, Charles Martel defeated an Umayyad raiding force."},{"prompt":"Charlemagne was crowned Emperor by Pope Leo III in the year…","options":["751 CE","800 CE","843 CE","910 CE"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The coronation took place on Christmas Day, 800 CE, reviving an imperial title in the west."},{"prompt":"The 843 CE Treaty of Verdun is significant because it…","options":["converted the Franks to Christianity","divided Charlemagne''s empire among his three grandsons","ended the Western Roman Empire","founded the monastery of Cluny"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Verdun split the Carolingian empire into three kingdoms, foreshadowing later France and Germany."},{"prompt":"A serf on a medieval manor was…","options":["a free knight who owned land","a peasant legally bound to the manor and its land","a merchant in a town","a monk under the Rule of Saint Benedict"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Serfs were unfree peasants tied to the manor, owing labor and crops in return for land and protection."},{"prompt":"The 1054 CE split between the Roman Catholic west and the Eastern Orthodox east is known as the…","options":["Investiture Controversy","Great Schism","Migration Period","Concordat of Worms"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Great Schism of 1054 CE divided Christianity into its Catholic and Orthodox branches."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (7, 'Byzantium and the Viking age', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe the Byzantine Empire that carried Roman civilization onward in the east, and explain the Viking raids and settlements that reshaped the north. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, what was the capital of the Byzantine Empire? After this lesson you will be able to name Justinian''s major achievements, explain what made Byzantium distinct from the Latin west, and describe where the Vikings raided and settled between the 790s and the 1000s CE. You will be able to argue that while the west fragmented, the Byzantine Empire preserved Roman law and Greek learning, and that the Vikings were not only raiders but also traders, explorers, and settlers who left a lasting mark from England to Russia to the edge of North America."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"When Rome fell in the west, the Eastern Roman Empire lived on from its capital, Constantinople. Historians call it Byzantine. It spoke Greek, followed Orthodox Christianity, and preserved Roman law. Its greatest early emperor, Justinian I (527-565 CE), reconquered parts of the west, built the vast church of Hagia Sophia, and had Roman law codified into the Corpus Juris Civilis, which shaped legal systems for centuries. Far to the north, from the 790s CE, Scandinavian seafarers known as Vikings burst outward. In fast longships they raided monasteries and towns, but they also traded across Russia, settled Iceland and Greenland, briefly reached North America around 1000 CE, and founded dukedoms and kingdoms. They were feared, but also skilled sailors, merchants, and colonists who transformed the places they touched."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"330 CE: Constantine founds Constantinople. 527-565 CE: Justinian I reconquers territory, builds Hagia Sophia (completed 537 CE), and codifies Roman law. The empire then endured waves of enemies for centuries. 793 CE: Vikings raid the monastery of Lindisfarne in England, a traditional start of the Viking Age. 871-899 CE: Alfred the Great of Wessex resists the Danes and secures English survival, later leading to the Danelaw boundary. 911 CE: the Frankish king grants land to the Viking leader Rollo, creating Normandy. c. 1000 CE: Leif Erikson reaches Vinland in North America. 1066 CE: the Norman conquest of England and the Norwegian defeat at Stamford Bridge mark a conventional end of the Viking Age. Magyars and Muslim raiders also pressed Europe in these centuries."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What language and branch of Christianity defined the Byzantine Empire? 2) Name two achievements of Justinian I. 3) What event in 793 CE is often used to mark the start of the Viking Age? 4) What region of France did the Vikings settle, and under which leader? 5) Who is traditionally said to have reached North America around 1000 CE? Answers: 1) Greek and Eastern Orthodox Christianity. 2) He reconquered western territory, built Hagia Sophia, and codified Roman law (any two). 3) The Viking raid on the monastery of Lindisfarne. 4) Normandy, under Rollo, around 911 CE. 5) The Norse explorer Leif Erikson."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming Rome''s fall ended Roman civilization everywhere — the Byzantine Empire carried it on in the east for nearly a thousand more years. Mistake: picturing Vikings only as raiders; they were also traders, explorers, and settlers. Mistake: forgetting that the Normans who later conquered England in 1066 CE descended from Vikings who had settled in France. Exit ticket: (a) Which emperor codified Roman law and built Hagia Sophia? (b) Give one way the Vikings shaped Europe besides raiding. Answers: (a) Justinian I. (b) They traded widely, settled Iceland and Normandy, explored the North Atlantic, or founded new states."}]}'::jsonb, 'Byzantine refers to the Eastern Roman Empire, centered on Constantinople. ''Norse'' and ''Viking'' both describe the seafaring peoples of Scandinavia in roughly 793-1066 CE.', 14),
  (8, 'The High Middle Ages: towns, trade, and learning', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain the surge of growth in the High Middle Ages (about 1000 to 1300 CE): rising population, reviving towns and trade, new guilds and universities, and the soaring Gothic cathedrals. Warm-up: the diagnostic asked what features define Gothic cathedrals — recall them. After this lesson you will be able to connect better farming to population growth, describe how towns and merchant guilds revived commerce, explain what universities were and why they appeared, and describe the Gothic style and the engineering that made it possible. You will be able to argue that these three centuries were among the most dynamic of the Middle Ages, turning a rural, local world toward towns, markets, schools, and grand building."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"After the year 1000 CE, Europe grew. Warmer weather and better methods — the heavy plow, the three-field system, and water and wind mills — raised food output, and the population climbed. Surplus food fed people who did not farm, so towns revived and trade spread along rivers, roads, and sea routes, from the Italian cities to the Hanseatic League of the north and the great Champagne fairs. In the towns, guilds organized merchants and craftsmen, set standards and prices, and trained apprentices. Learning grew too: universities appeared at Bologna, Paris, and Oxford, teaching law, medicine, and theology, where scholars used reasoning (scholasticism) to harmonize faith with the newly recovered works of Aristotle. And faith reached for the sky in Gothic cathedrals, whose pointed arches, ribbed vaults, and flying buttresses let walls dissolve into glowing stained glass."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"c. 1000-1300 CE: population, towns, and trade all expand across Europe. 1088 CE: the University of Bologna is founded, often called the oldest university, soon followed by Paris and Oxford. Around 1140 CE, Abbot Suger rebuilds the choir of Saint-Denis near Paris, usually seen as the birth of the Gothic style; Notre-Dame of Paris and Chartres cathedral follow. The scholar Thomas Aquinas (1225-1274 CE) becomes the great figure of scholasticism, blending Aristotle with Christian theology. Merchant and craft guilds govern urban economic life, while the Hanseatic League links trading cities around the Baltic and North Sea. These were centuries of confidence and expansion, reflected in stone, commerce, and thought alike, and they rested on the quiet revolution of better farming in the countryside."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name one farming improvement that helped the population grow after 1000 CE. 2) What did guilds do? 3) Name one of the earliest medieval universities. 4) What three architectural features define Gothic cathedrals? 5) Who was the leading scholar of scholasticism? Answers: 1) The heavy plow, the three-field system, or water and wind mills. 2) They organized merchants or craftsmen, set standards and prices, and trained apprentices. 3) Bologna, Paris, or Oxford. 4) Pointed arches, ribbed vaults, and flying buttresses (with large stained-glass windows). 5) Thomas Aquinas."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking medieval Europe was always stagnant — the High Middle Ages were a time of marked growth in people, towns, trade, and learning. Mistake: dating universities to the Renaissance; the first appeared in the 1000s and 1100s CE. Mistake: confusing Gothic with Romanesque; Gothic is lighter and taller, with pointed arches and flying buttresses, while Romanesque is heavier with rounded arches and thick walls. Exit ticket: (a) What broad trend in population marks the High Middle Ages? (b) What engineering feature let Gothic walls open up for stained glass? Answers: (a) Population grew substantially. (b) The flying buttress, which carried the roof''s weight outward to external supports."}]}'::jsonb, 'The High Middle Ages span roughly 1000-1300 CE, a time of growth. A guild is an association of merchants or craftsmen that regulated a trade.', 14),
  (9, 'Crusades, kings, and the Magna Carta', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain the Crusades and their consequences, and describe how kings and nobles struggled over power, producing landmarks like the Magna Carta of 1215. Warm-up: the diagnostic asked who called for the recovery of Jerusalem in 1095 — recall the answer. After this lesson you will be able to say why the Crusades began, name a few of their results, and explain how the conflict between English kings and their barons led to Magna Carta and the growth of representative assemblies. You will be able to argue that these centuries both projected European power outward, into the eastern Mediterranean, and began to limit royal power at home, planting seeds of law-bound government and parliaments."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"In 1095 CE, at the Council of Clermont, Pope Urban II called on Christians to recapture Jerusalem and the Holy Land from Muslim rule. The response launched the Crusades, a series of armed expeditions over roughly two centuries. The First Crusade took Jerusalem in 1099 CE and set up crusader states, but Muslim forces gradually recovered the territory, and later crusades often failed; the Fourth Crusade even sacked Christian Constantinople in 1204 CE. Meanwhile, within kingdoms, rulers tried to strengthen central power while nobles resisted. In England this came to a head when barons forced King John to seal Magna Carta in 1215 CE, limiting the king and affirming that even a monarch was subject to law. From such disputes grew assemblies that would become parliaments."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"1095 CE: Pope Urban II launches the First Crusade at Clermont. 1099 CE: the crusaders capture Jerusalem and found crusader states. 1187 CE: Saladin retakes Jerusalem, prompting the Third Crusade. 1204 CE: the Fourth Crusade sacks Constantinople. 1291 CE: the fall of Acre ends the main crusader presence in the Holy Land. Military orders such as the Knights Templar and Hospitaller arise to fight and to protect pilgrims. In England, 1215 CE: King John seals Magna Carta (''Great Charter'') at Runnymede. 1265 CE: Simon de Montfort summons an early parliament including commoners, and by 1295 CE the ''Model Parliament'' becomes a pattern. Trade and ideas also flowed back from the east, enriching Europe even as the campaigns mostly failed militarily in the long run."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Who called the First Crusade, and in what year? 2) What did the First Crusade achieve in 1099 CE? 3) What shocking thing did the Fourth Crusade do in 1204 CE? 4) What did Magna Carta (1215 CE) establish in principle? 5) Why do the Crusades matter even though they ultimately failed to hold the Holy Land? Answers: 1) Pope Urban II, in 1095 CE. 2) It captured Jerusalem and set up crusader states. 3) It sacked the Christian city of Constantinople. 4) That the king was not above the law and must respect certain rights. 5) They spurred trade, contact, and the exchange of ideas between Europe and the eastern Mediterranean."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking the Crusades were a single war — there were many over about two centuries, with mixed and often unsuccessful results. Mistake: assuming all crusades targeted Muslims; the Fourth Crusade attacked fellow Christians at Constantinople. Mistake: treating Magna Carta as instant democracy — it chiefly protected barons, but its principle that the ruler is bound by law proved hugely influential. Exit ticket: (a) In what year was Magna Carta sealed, and by which king? (b) Name one lasting effect of the Crusades on Europe. Answers: (a) 1215 CE, by King John. (b) Expanded trade and the exchange of goods and ideas with the east (or the rise of military orders)."}]}'::jsonb, 'The Crusades were religious wars (1095 onward). A charter is a formal written grant of rights; the Magna Carta of 1215 is the most famous.', 14),
  (10, 'Crisis and rebirth: the Late Middle Ages', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain the crises that shook Europe in the Late Middle Ages — famine, plague, and a long war — and describe how this battered world gave way to the Renaissance. Warm-up: the diagnostic named the pandemic of around 1347-1351 — recall its name. After this lesson you will be able to describe the Black Death and its effects, summarize the Hundred Years'' War between England and France, and explain how disruption helped open the path to the Renaissance and the early modern age. You will be able to argue that catastrophe reshaped medieval society in lasting ways, weakening serfdom, shaking the Church''s authority, and clearing space for new ideas, art, and the technology of the printing press."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The 1300s opened badly. The Great Famine of 1315-1317 CE brought mass hunger, and in 1347-1351 CE the Black Death, a wave of plague, killed perhaps a third to half of Europe''s people. Society reeled: with so few workers left, surviving peasants could demand better terms, and serfdom weakened in much of the west. Faith was tested as the plague struck rich and poor alike, and the Church''s prestige suffered further during the Western Schism (1378-1417 CE), when rival popes claimed the throne. Across these same years England and France fought the Hundred Years'' War (1337-1453 CE), a long dynastic struggle marked by figures like Joan of Arc. Out of this turmoil, and building on the growth of the earlier High Middle Ages, a new cultural movement arose in Italy: the Renaissance, a rebirth of interest in classical learning, art, and human potential."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"1315-1317 CE: the Great Famine. 1337-1453 CE: the Hundred Years'' War between England and France, including English victories at Crecy (1346 CE) and Agincourt (1415 CE), and the rise of Joan of Arc, who rallied France before her execution in 1431 CE. 1347-1351 CE: the Black Death devastates Europe. 1378-1417 CE: the Western Schism divides the Church among rival popes. 1381 CE: the English Peasants'' Revolt reflects social unrest. c. 1440-1450 CE: Johannes Gutenberg develops printing with movable type in Europe, spreading books and ideas rapidly. 1453 CE: Constantinople falls to the Ottoman Turks, ending the Byzantine Empire and marking, for many historians, the close of the Middle Ages and the dawn of the early modern world."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What was the Black Death, and roughly when did it strike? 2) How did the huge loss of life affect peasants and serfdom? 3) Which two kingdoms fought the Hundred Years'' War? 4) What did Gutenberg develop around 1440-1450 CE? 5) What 1453 CE event is often used to mark the end of the Middle Ages? Answers: 1) A plague pandemic around 1347-1351 CE that killed perhaps a third to half of Europe''s population. 2) Labor became scarce, so survivors could demand better terms, and serfdom weakened in much of the west. 3) England and France. 4) Printing with movable type, which spread books and ideas. 5) The fall of Constantinople to the Ottoman Turks."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking the Black Death only killed people and changed nothing else — it reshaped labor, wages, serfdom, and faith. Mistake: imagining the Renaissance appeared from nowhere; it grew out of the towns, trade, and learning of the High Middle Ages and the upheavals of the Late Middle Ages. Mistake: dating the Hundred Years'' War loosely; it ran 1337-1453 CE and was not a single continuous battle. Exit ticket: (a) About what fraction of Europe''s population may the Black Death have killed? (b) Name one reason the Late Middle Ages are seen as a turning point toward the modern era. Answers: (a) Roughly a third to a half. (b) The weakening of serfdom, the shaken Church, the printing press, or the fall of Constantinople in 1453 CE."}]}'::jsonb, 'The Late Middle Ages span roughly 1300-1500 CE. The Renaissance (''rebirth'') is the cultural movement that followed, beginning in Italy in the 1300s CE.', 15)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-00000000000e' and l.position = v.pos
);
